            Triple("Cost-Utility Analysis (CUA) measures health outcomes in standardized composite utility metrics known as:", listOf("Monetary rupees", "Quality-Adjusted Life Years (QALYs) or Disability-Adjusted Life Years (DALYs)", "Bed days", "Drug doses"), 1),
            Triple("One Quality-Adjusted Life Year (1 QALY) represents:", listOf("One year of life with severe disability", "One year of life lived in full, perfect health", "Ten years of disease", "One hospital admission"), 1),
            Triple("Disability-Adjusted Life Year (1 DALY) quantifies the loss of the equivalent of:", listOf("One year of perfect health due to premature death or disability (DALY = YLL + YLD)", "One year of employment", "One hospital bed day", "One doctor visit"), 0),
            Triple("Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio (ICER) formula calculates the additional cost per additional unit of health outcome gained when comparing new treatment to standard care:", listOf("ICER = (Cost B - Cost A) / (Effect B - Effect A)", "ICER = Cost A x Effect A", "ICER = Total Cost / Total Patients", "ICER = Profit / Loss"), 0),
            Triple("Gross Domestic Product (GDP) percentage allocated to total health expenditure in Pakistan historically ranges around:", listOf("15% to 20%", "Less than 1.5% to 2.0% of GDP (below WHO recommended 5%)", "10%", "30%"), 1),
            Triple("Out-Of-Pocket (OOP) expenditure accounts for approximately what percentage of total health spending in Pakistan, exposing families to catastrophic health impoverishment?", listOf("10%", "Over 60% to 65% of total health expenditure", "25%", "90%"), 1),
            Triple("Sehat Sahulat Program (Sehat Card Plus KP) in Khyber Pakhtunkhwa provides micro-health insurance coverage for inpatient hospital care up to Rs.:", listOf("100,000 per family", "1,000,000 (10 Lakh) Rupees per family per year", "50,000 per family", "5,000,000 per family"), 1),
            Triple("Sehat Card Plus KP covers secondary and tertiary medical treatments including cardiac surgery, oncology, renal dialysis, and neurosurgery for enrolled families across:", listOf("Selected rich citizens only", "100% of permanent resident families of KP", "Government employees only", "Urban areas only"), 1),
            Triple("Health Policy is defined as formal decisions, plans, and actions undertaken by governmental or organizational bodies to achieve specific:", listOf("Commercial profit goals", "Healthcare goals within a society", "Tax collection increases", "Political campaign targets"), 1),
            Triple("Policy Making Cycle initial phase involves identifying public issues and placing them on the government's priority list, termed:", listOf("Policy Adoption", "Agenda Setting", "Policy Implementation", "Policy Evaluation"), 1),
            Triple("Second phase of Policy Making Cycle where policy options, legislation drafts, and proposals are developed is:", listOf("Agenda Setting", "Policy Formulation", "Policy Evaluation", "Policy Termination"), 1),
            Triple("Policy Implementation stage of policy process involves:", listOf("Drafting bills", "Translating enacted laws and policies into operational guidelines, programs, and administrative actions", "Evaluating past laws", "Campaigning"), 1),
            Triple("Universal Health Coverage (UHC) core objective defined by WHO ensures all people receive quality health services without suffering:", listOf("Any minor illness", "Financial hardship when paying for care", "Hospital queues", "Need for prescription"), 1),
            Triple("UHC 'Cube' model framework evaluates coverage across three dimensions: Population coverage (who is covered), Service coverage (which services are covered), and:", listOf("Private clinic growth", "Financial protection (what proportion of costs are covered)", "Doctor salaries", "Hospital building size"), 1),
            Triple("Single-Payer Healthcare System (e.g. UK National Health Service) is financed primarily through general taxation where the government acts as the:", listOf("Private insurer", "Single public payer funding health services for all citizens", "Commercial bank", "Hospital owner"), 1),
            Triple("Social Health Insurance model (Bismarck model e.g. Germany) finances healthcare through mandatory payroll contributions deducted from employees and employers into dedicated non-profit:", listOf("Private stock funds", "Sickness Funds / Health Funds", "Tax revenue", "Personal savings"), 1),
            Triple("Market-Driven Private Health Insurance model (e.g. US predominant model) relies on voluntary purchase of health insurance from commercial for-profit or non-profit insurers, leading to:", listOf("100% equal coverage", "Significant population coverage gaps and high administrative costs", "Lowest health costs globally", "Zero un-insured"), 1),
            Triple("Supply and Demand imbalance in nursing workforce leading to nurse shortages causes wage increases and increased reliance on high-cost:", listOf("Permanent staff", "Temporary agency / Travel Nurses and overtime pay", "Student nurses", "Midwives"), 1),
            Triple("Cost-Containment strategies in hospital operations include standardized clinical pathways, bulk purchasing of generic pharmaceuticals, and reducing patient:", listOf("Nursing care quality", "Unnecessary length of hospital stay (LOS) and hospital-acquired complications", "Hand hygiene", "Oxygen flow"), 1),
            Triple("Length of Stay (LOS) reduction without increasing 30-day hospital readmission rates is a key operational efficiency metric managed by nursing:", listOf("Finance officers", "Case Managers and Discharge Planners", "Janitors", "Security staff"), 1),
            Triple("Hospital-Acquired Conditions (HACs) such as catheter-associated urinary tract infections (CAUTI) and central line infections (CLABSI) under value-based purchasing result in financial:", listOf("Bonus payments to hospital", "Non-reimbursement or financial penalties imposed on the hospital", "Tax exemptions", "Free equipment"), 1),
            Triple("Direct Medical Costs in health economics evaluation include expenses directly incurred for clinical care such as:", listOf("Lost work wages", "Medications, diagnostic tests, hospital bed stay, and nursing care fees", "Travel cost to clinic", "Family caregiver time"), 1),
            Triple("Indirect Medical Costs account for economic losses resulting from patient illness including lost productivity, absenteeism, and:", listOf("Doctor consultation fee", "Premature mortality and loss of earning capacity", "Syringe price", "X-ray charge"), 1),
